WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.780
JONATHAN SHEFFI: Hi, and
welcome to Cloud onAir,

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.390
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:13.730
We're hosting webinars
every Tuesday.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.010
My name's Jonathan Sheffi.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:17.570
I'm the product manager for
Genomics and Life Sciences

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:19.190
at Google Cloud.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.600
And today we're talking about
powering genomics research

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.520
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.680
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform,

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.000
and we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:28.750
Let's get started.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.307
So a few topics we're
going to talk about today.

00:00:34.307 --> 00:00:36.390
First I'm going to tell
you a little bit about how

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.450
you can accomplish genomic and
clinical data mining together

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.802
using BigQuery.

00:00:40.802 --> 00:00:42.510
Then I'll tell you a
little bit about how

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:47.089
you can run batch workflows
at scale using Pipelines API.

00:00:47.089 --> 00:00:49.630
Then we'll hand it over to my
friend Geraldine from the Broad

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:53.290
Institute to tell you a bit
about GATK4 and the FireCloud

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:54.440
platform.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.340
She'll also tell you about
how their team optimized

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.540
GATK4 for Google Cloud.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.302
And then Keith from
Google will tell you

00:01:02.302 --> 00:01:04.260
a bit about some of the
really cool innovations

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.890
that are happening
right here at GCP.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.377
But first, I want to tell you
a little bit about the team

00:01:11.377 --> 00:01:12.690
that I'm a part of.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.620
This is a team that started
at Google just last year.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.330
It's called the Cloud for
Health and Life Sciences team.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:22.060
We brought together product
managers, software engineers,

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:25.090
solution architects,
and a commercial team

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.610
to help organizations
do all sorts

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.830
of amazing things with
health and life sciences data

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:31.540
using Google Cloud.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.190
Whether it's an
academic institution,

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:38.950
whether it's a pharma company,
a provider, a payer, a health

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:41.165
products or services
company, we want

00:01:41.165 --> 00:01:43.870
to help you use any kind of
health and life science data

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:48.780
type in Google Cloud
more effectively.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.810
In particular, the product
teams that I'm a part of

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.660
are focused on a
few key modalities--

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.620
clinical data, so
EMR data, EHR data,

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.290
other kinds of
phenotypic datasets;

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:08.160
imaging data, that's DICOM,
PACS, PET scans, MRIs, X-rays;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.882
and my team, the Genomics
and Life Sciences team,

00:02:10.882 --> 00:02:12.840
where we're dealing with
not only genomic data,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.450
but all kinds of other
biomedical datasets.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.230
So it could be
microbiome, RNA-seq,

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.870
and others, flow cytometry.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.630
Why we're doing this is because
our vision is that there's

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.810
going to be a convergence of
these data types in the cloud,

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.250
where for whatever sample
you're working with,

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:34.500
whether it's yeast or an
entire human population,

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.410
it will be helpful for you
to be able to analyze data

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.630
across these different
modalities together.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.780
And that's what we're enabling.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.620
So let's talk a bit about what
that can look like, especially

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.995
coming from the genomics and
life sciences point of view.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.110
So one of the
products from my team

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.640
is called Variant Transforms.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.310
Variant Transforms is
an open source tool

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:02.030
to help you manage your
genomic data using BigQuery.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:06.730
You can think about it as a way
to take your VCFs from panels,

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:10.520
exomes, or genomes and import
them directly into BigQuery

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.550
using the inherent VCF scheme
that you're presenting.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.070
From there, you can do
things like calculate

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:19.280
your allele frequency,
or build a cohort,

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.970
or do ad hoc exploration
of that dataset.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.390
But I think we all know that
where these kinds of datasets

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.520
get really exciting
is when we join them

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.390
with other kinds of data.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.910
So that's exactly why a company
called Color in the Bay Area

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:33.410
did.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:37.300
And I want to tell you a
little bit more about that.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.790
So Color is a health
services company.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.900
They offer very affordable and
accessible genetic testing.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.860
And they have a 30
gene panel in cancer.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.580
And they also have an existing
database of phenotypic data,

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.700
some health history
data, and report data

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.906
that was sitting on
Postgres database.

00:03:56.906 --> 00:03:58.280
The problem they
had is that they

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.580
wanted to be able to expand that
test by mining the raw sequence

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.310
data alongside the other data
they had available on those

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:05.960
samples.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.910
The cloud provider they'd
been using up till now

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:11.550
didn't really have a mature
enough managed data warehouse

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.210
solution like BigQuery, which
is so great for these kinds

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:16.769
of scalable queries.

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:21.269
So what'd they do?

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:23.520
They used Variant
Transforms-- as I mentioned,

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.250
totally open source tool--
for bringing all of that data

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:27.510
into BigQuery.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:29.910
They took the data from the
Postgres database, which

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.710
had a bunch of
biomarker information,

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.799
and put that in BigQuery
and joined it together.

00:04:34.799 --> 00:04:36.340
On top of that,
their data scientists

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:39.330
stood up all kinds of really
cool machine learning tools

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.930
and built a whole
bunch of great models.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:44.870
So what happened?

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:48.650
Well, they got all the feature
engineering done in one week.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:50.570
And they created
three novel models

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:53.210
that outperformed an
existing major clinical model

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:55.580
for predicting
certain biomarkers.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:59.990
In fact, their test, which is
actually designed for cancer,

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.030
they found out worked in
a completely unrelated

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:03.260
therapeutic area.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:04.480
This is really exciting.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.070
It may become a publication,
it may become a novel test.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:09.350
But we're really excited
about the kinds of results

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:11.810
that our customers are
achieving by joining

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:17.562
their genomic data with other
data types using BigQuery.

00:05:17.562 --> 00:05:19.270
So I'm going to switch
gears for a minute

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:23.110
here from tertiary analysis
to secondary analysis.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:24.620
Your data, if you're
watching this,

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:28.030
you're probably processing
lots of samples,

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:30.370
perhaps in cloud environment,
perhaps somewhere else.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:32.578
And you're thinking about
how you can really scale up

00:05:32.578 --> 00:05:36.890
that work of your pipelines.

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:40.840
So our team also
offers an API called,

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.430
not surprisingly, Pipelines API.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:46.160
Pipelines API helps you run
bioinformatics pipelines

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:50.540
at scale and achieving great
metrics around turnaround time

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:51.860
and cost.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.980
You can stand up all kinds of
great industry standard tools,

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:57.860
like GATK, which you're going
to be hearing more about.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:00.780
And you can optimize
for parallel execution.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.960
And you can leverage a
lot of great innovations

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.120
in Google Cloud, like
preemptible virtual machines,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:07.287
GPUs, regional storage.

00:06:07.287 --> 00:06:08.870
You're going to hear
a little bit more

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.310
about that later today.

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:13.490
We've also brought together
a phenomenal ecosystem

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:16.310
of platform partners
and workflow engines

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:17.870
to help deal with all this.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:19.670
So soon you're
going to hear more

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.250
about Broad Institute and
their FireCloud platform,

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:25.190
and you'll also hear about the
Cromwell open source workflow

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:27.650
engine, which are great ways
of running your workflows

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:30.060
and leveraging the
power of Pipelines API.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.450
We also have other partners,
such as WuXi NextCODE,

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:36.080
DNAstack, Seven Bridges, and the
Nextflow open source workflow

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:36.580
engine.

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:42.200
The kinds of results
that people get

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:46.110
from leveraging Pipelines API
is this kind of massive scale.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:49.070
So for example, the team at
Stanford School of Medicine

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:52.520
is being able to run thousands
of genomes in parallel, what

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:55.376
today they say feels
like infinite resources,

00:06:55.376 --> 00:06:57.500
and being able to boot up
a lot of machines at once

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:00.180
to accomplish a batch workflow
in a very short period of time,

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.180
while still focusing
on low cost.

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:09.180
Our friends at the Broad have
used Pipelines API quite a bit,

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.720
shall we say.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.770
They process over 250
genomes every single day.

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:17.440
They are now working
with over 35 petabytes

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:18.750
of data in Google Cloud.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:21.850
And just last year,
consumed over 100 million

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:23.862
compute core hours.

00:07:23.862 --> 00:07:26.320
And they're scaling up their
work because of the innovation

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:27.370
within Pipelines API.

00:07:30.019 --> 00:07:32.060
So let's talk a little
bit about some of the work

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:33.340
that they're doing.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:36.510
The Broad gnomAD team
recently called Variants

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:41.425
on the gnomAD dataset
with the GRCh38 reference.

00:07:41.425 --> 00:07:44.510
And the problem they had was
that gnomAD, as you might know,

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:47.660
it's 79,000 whole genomes
that were previously

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:49.515
called with HD19.

00:07:49.515 --> 00:07:51.140
They wanted to be
able to call variants

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:56.450
for that entire data set
using GATK4 and GRCh38.

00:07:56.450 --> 00:08:00.440
So they were able to
spin up 79,000 workflows

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:05.180
and call the variants for the
entire dataset in three days.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:06.680
It's a really
exciting result, and I

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:08.638
think it's a great example
of the kind of scale

00:08:08.638 --> 00:08:14.000
you can achieve using
Pipelines API and engines

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.012
such as Cromwell and
platforms such as FireCloud.

00:08:18.012 --> 00:08:19.220
So what's next for that team?

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:20.719
They're going to
be calling variants

00:08:20.719 --> 00:08:23.600
for exome data on
gnomAD as well, again,

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:28.220
using some of the same
technologies and techniques.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:31.310
So speaking of innovations
with Pipelines API,

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:33.650
just last month, we
released a brand new version

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:36.890
of Pipelines API for our
ecosystem of platform partners

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:38.240
and workflow engines.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:39.320
What's new?

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:41.809
Well, first you're able
to run multiple Docker

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:42.989
containers per instance.

00:08:42.989 --> 00:08:44.780
This is really helpful,
because you can now

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:46.700
run service containers
in the background

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:49.650
while the workflow is
running in progress.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.770
We also make it a lot
easier for tool authors

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:55.520
to be able to inject their own
containers around a workflow.

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.800
Localization and delocalization
of files is much, much simpler.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.430
It's just now a
container instruction.

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:03.920
And there's also-- also
my favorite feature--

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:06.350
a lot more information is
available to our platform

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:08.840
partners and workflow
engine partners

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:12.620
on the information about the
transition between states

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:13.560
and on failure modes.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:16.190
So for example, when I
have a pipeline that fails,

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:17.270
why did it fail?

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:18.350
Did it run out of memory?

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:19.280
Did it simply crash?

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:20.988
Did something happen
to the node somehow?

00:09:20.988 --> 00:09:22.274
Did I get preempted?

00:09:22.274 --> 00:09:24.440
That information was a
little hard to access before.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:27.770
And now it's much easier and
provides a better experience

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:30.890
for whatever workflow engine
or platform you're using.

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:35.450
We've also made it easier to
specify regions and zones,

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:38.589
and much easier to specify
minimum CPU platform options.

00:09:38.589 --> 00:09:40.130
Keith will tell you
a little bit more

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:43.700
about that soon in terms of how
you can bring up custom VMs.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:49.160
So all this great talk
about secondary analysis--

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:51.740
let's learn a little bit
more about how people are

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:53.280
doing this in the real world.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:55.946
So now I'm going to invite up my
friend Geraldine from the Broad

00:09:55.946 --> 00:09:58.663
to tell you more about
GATK4 and FireCloud.

00:10:03.404 --> 00:10:05.320
GERALDINE A. VAN DER
AUWERA: Thanks, Jonathan.

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:06.830
It's good to be here.

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:10.690
I'm going to talk today
about GATK4 and FireCloud,

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:14.230
and the way we have
been working to make

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:17.680
our pipelines
available, accessible

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:20.800
to the wider community
of researchers.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:24.520
Now, first I want to
start with a little bit

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:28.930
of a recap of what is GATK
exactly, what that covers.

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:32.560
So GATK is a genomic
analysis toolkit.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:38.360
And this is roughly the
process for genomic analysis,

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:41.565
specifically with a focus
on variant discovery.

00:10:41.565 --> 00:10:42.940
If you think that
you're starting

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:47.170
with biological material,
for example, blood samples,

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:50.140
you want to extract
DNA from that, put that

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:53.440
through the sequencing machine,
that's going to produce

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:57.040
a huge pile of short reads.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:59.320
Those short reads,
that's the raw data

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:03.670
that we're going to be
using in our pipelines.

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:05.500
The end result that
we want to get to

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:11.950
is a list of variation mutations
in the individual genome

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:14.660
or genomes of a
group of individuals.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:20.710
And so the pipeline, which
is what GATK constitutes,

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:25.480
is going to take that raw
data, do some cleanup,

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:28.245
do some mapping to find
out where the reads belong

00:11:28.245 --> 00:11:31.660
in the genome, and then
do the analysis that

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:36.040
enables us to identify those
variations, those mutations

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:40.010
in individual genomes,
which is ultimately what

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:42.400
the researcher cares about.

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:44.980
Now, these tools have been
around for a long time,

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:48.550
about a decade, which
honestly, in genomics time,

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:50.780
pretty much like a century.

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:53.530
And for a long time, they were
evolving kind of naturally.

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:59.890
We're just improving
our ability to just push

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:02.800
the scientific boundaries
of what we could identify.

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:05.740
Now, over the last few
years, as Jonathan mentioned,

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:09.340
there's been a huge
explosion in the size

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:11.940
of the genomic data
and the datasets

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:14.280
that we need to be
able to analyze.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:16.780
And so we've put a lot
of work into making sure

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:18.670
that the tools and
the pipelines can

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:24.760
cope with that huge explosion
in the size of datasets.

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:26.560
And specifically,
we built what we

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:30.980
call GATK4, which is version
4 of the GATK toolkit.

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:33.460
I'm not going to go
into a detailed laundry

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:36.520
list of features,
but the main themes

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:40.790
are that we've really improved
the technical performance,

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:43.440
the speed and
scalability of the tools.

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:45.400
And that has involved
a complete rewrite.

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:48.820
We've rewritten that entire
software code base from scratch

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:51.490
to be able to cope
with the demands

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:58.750
of the genomic workloads
that are now today's reality.

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:03.460
But it's not just about
the technical performance,

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:05.420
although that's very important.

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:07.990
But we've also put a lot
of work into expanding

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:12.580
the scope of analysis to be able
to cover more variant types.

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:17.500
So where previously we were
very focused on germ line short

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:20.230
variants-- that was kind
of what we were known for--

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:24.980
now we've expanded that to cover
all the major variant classes,

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:27.820
including copy number,
structural variation

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:31.510
in both germline and
somatic mutations.

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:37.210
So we're now able to cover
almost all of these use cases.

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:41.230
And the remaining one that is
not quite implemented today,

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:44.110
somatic structural
variation, is planned.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:46.060
We have that on the roadmap.

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:48.120
We hope to deliver
that later this year.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:51.010
And so the idea is that you
have all these pipelines that

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:56.320
allow you to really do all the
scientific work that you need

00:13:56.320 --> 00:13:58.480
related to variant discovery.

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:01.150
Now, the thing is--

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:05.980
and there's been two big
pushes in that direction.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:09.130
One is just developing
the right methods

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:15.520
to produce those end results,
but also improve the speed

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:18.400
and performance of the tools.

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:24.860
And that has involved leveraging
a lot of the improvements in,

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:28.450
for example, machine learning
that have really flourished

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:30.410
over the past few years.

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:33.580
Now, if we look at these
pipelines, all of them

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:37.780
involve a sizable
number of steps

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:39.100
involving different tools.

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:40.750
And for the individual
researchers,

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:45.730
it's not easy to
implement this at scale.

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:48.940
And so one of the things
we've been focused on lately

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:52.180
is formalizing
pipeline definitions,

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:55.110
which we call the GATK
best practices, which

00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:58.090
a researcher can take
and apply to their data

00:14:58.090 --> 00:15:02.200
without having to figure
out, by themselves,

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:03.830
how to implement this.

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:07.250
So we call that the GATK
best practices pipelines.

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:10.120
But there's still a big step
between having the pipeline

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:14.360
definitions and being
able to implement that.

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:15.760
And so we've gone
a step further,

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:19.330
and now we're making available
these pipelines as ready

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:27.040
to run pipelines that are
preloaded in a platform

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:29.840
that we call FireCloud,
which runs on Google Cloud.

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:33.670
And so we have these workspaces
that contain the pipeline,

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:35.360
as well as example data.

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:39.340
And so anyone can go and
clone one of those workspaces,

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:42.640
run the pipeline as it is
preconfigured on the example

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:43.510
data.

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:47.020
They can also upload
their own data

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:49.330
and run the pipeline
on their own data.

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:52.900
And that way you have the
GATK best practices pipelines

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:56.747
just available in ready to
run state out of the box.

00:15:56.747 --> 00:15:58.330
So hopefully, I've
got you interested.

00:15:58.330 --> 00:16:00.040
Now I want to tell
you a little bit more

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:04.300
about FireCloud's
platform itself.

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:08.200
OK, so FireCloud is meant to
be a self-service analysis

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:10.780
portal that anybody can access.

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:13.720
And specifically, anybody
can access for free.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:16.600
So we built this originally
for Broad researchers,

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:19.330
but we made this
available for free.

00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:21.960
Of course, it's using
Google Cloud resources,

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:25.450
so you still have to pay
Google Cloud for storage

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:27.650
and compute cost,
things like that.

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:29.710
But we do have a
free credits program

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:33.190
that you can use to get
started with the platform

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:38.260
to test it out without taking
any financial risk yourself.

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:42.290
Now, the platform itself
offers a number of services,

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:44.560
including the ability
to manage your data,

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:51.590
to share your data securely, and
to run workflows on your data.

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:53.920
So we've got a
number of pipelines

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:57.730
that are preset that you can run
on your data in this platform,

00:16:57.730 --> 00:17:00.160
but you can also bring
your own pipelines,

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:03.936
either variations of ours,
or just different workloads

00:17:03.936 --> 00:17:04.810
that you need to run.

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:07.990
Because we understand
that many people

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:10.740
use other tools besides GATK.

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:14.250
And we can use this through
the web portal, which

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:16.640
is a graphical user interface.

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:18.280
You can also use
it through the API

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:20.740
if you prefer to
interact programmatically

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:22.790
with the platform.

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:26.020
And as of last week, we
also have, now, the ability

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:29.990
to spin up Jupyter
notebooks on smart clusters,

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:34.370
so you can follow-up your
batch execution, your pipeline

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:37.886
execution with interactive
analysis of the results

00:17:37.886 --> 00:17:38.635
that they produce.

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:43.990
Now, let's look
a little bit more

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:47.080
closely at how batch
execution of workloads

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:50.350
works in FireCloud.

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:53.620
Don't worry about the
details yet, but let's

00:17:53.620 --> 00:17:55.060
look at the overall system.

00:17:55.060 --> 00:17:59.320
Here we have two researchers who
have access to this workspace.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:05.140
The workspace
encapsulates everything

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:07.430
that's involved in a
particular project,

00:18:07.430 --> 00:18:10.000
including the definition
of the data that's involved

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.100
and the methods.

00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:14.200
And that's also the
place where you're going

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:16.780
to be executing pipelines.

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:20.910
So you also have access to
pipelines, the workflows.

00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:25.090
That's the stacked scripts that
you see above the workspace.

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:29.660
And you have a number of data
buckets that are available.

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:32.020
Now, the way it works is
that the data is stored

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:33.610
in the buckets on Google Cloud.

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:36.550
So your data is going to
live in Google Storage,

00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:39.730
either associated with the
workspace or in other buckets.

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:42.880
You can also compute on
data that's elsewhere

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:45.680
in the data library, which
are public datasets that

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:47.810
are provided.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:50.720
The workflows are WIDL scripts.

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:53.760
WIDL is a workflow
definition language.

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:57.070
The GATK pipelines
are all provided

00:18:57.070 --> 00:19:00.270
as preloaded WIDL scripts.

00:19:00.270 --> 00:19:04.180
And that contains the definition
of your actual workflows.

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:09.100
Now, when you go to run
a workflow on your data,

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:12.070
all you need to do, really,
is connect the workflow

00:19:12.070 --> 00:19:13.240
with the inputs.

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:14.950
The inputs, that's
the data that's

00:19:14.950 --> 00:19:17.140
defined in your
data model, which

00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:19.030
describes what files
you have available

00:19:19.030 --> 00:19:22.640
and how they plug
into the workflow.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:25.220
So you have your workflow.

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:27.020
You have your data definition.

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:30.640
You're going to launch one of
these workflows on your data.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:34.060
That's going to be handed off
on the backend to Cromwell,

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:35.830
which is the execution engine.

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:39.470
And Cromwell is going to take
care of everything for you.

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:41.230
So it's going to
execute your workflow,

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:45.440
talk to the Pipelines API,
which Jonathan talked about,

00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:48.670
and run your workflow on
the Google Compute Engine,

00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:54.200
and then write back the outputs
to your workspace data bucket.

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:59.940
So that's how you run a
pipeline in FireCloud.

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:03.370
And specifically, if you
you're using one of the GATK

00:20:03.370 --> 00:20:06.540
best practices pipelines
which is preloaded,

00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:10.710
you can get started
with this very quickly.

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:13.470
And it abstracts away
all the difficulties

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:18.210
of implementing these kind
of complicated pipelines

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:20.220
on the cloud.

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:23.460
And what's really neat is,
like I said, as of last week,

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:28.110
you can follow up and use
the results that you have.

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:32.670
Now you can explore your
results interactively

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:34.020
in Jupyter notebooks.

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:42.050
So that shows you how to
access and run the GATK4

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:44.570
pipelines in FireCloud.

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:46.720
Let's talk a little bit
about what it costs,

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:49.690
because when we started
migrating our workflows

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:53.890
to the Google platform,
it became clear to us

00:20:53.890 --> 00:20:56.890
that there were some great
opportunities to cut down

00:20:56.890 --> 00:20:59.450
the cost of operation.

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:01.570
And here I'm going
to talk briefly

00:21:01.570 --> 00:21:03.790
about the various
strategies that we

00:21:03.790 --> 00:21:07.830
used to bring down dramatically
the cost of operating

00:21:07.830 --> 00:21:10.360
our genome analysis pipeline.

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:16.210
Now, the main strategies
that we used--

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:19.600
and we developed
this with quite a lot

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:24.250
of friends, quite a lot of help
from our friends at Google--

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:27.980
have been in several
different categories.

00:21:27.980 --> 00:21:33.080
One was this idea of task
splitting and persistent disk.

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:35.890
Now, the idea here
is that when you

00:21:35.890 --> 00:21:39.640
have your tasks in
a workflow, you're

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:41.830
allocating for each task.

00:21:41.830 --> 00:21:44.530
You're allocating some
resources, some disk

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:49.400
size, some processor size.

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:53.170
Though the beefier the
machine, the bigger the disk,

00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:56.620
the more you're going to
pay for this hardware.

00:21:56.620 --> 00:21:58.840
And so what you want
to do is make sure

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:03.460
that the allocation is
commensurate with the needs

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:04.630
of your task.

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:07.390
And we have found that
there are various ways

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:12.310
that we can optimize how tasks
are either split up or bundled

00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:15.040
together, depending on
what their computational

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:19.120
requirements are, and what
is the size of the inputs

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:22.270
and outputs that we
need to store locally

00:22:22.270 --> 00:22:27.880
to really optimize the
allocation resource requests.

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:32.722
Now, the other-- and that
kind of goes with it.

00:22:32.722 --> 00:22:34.680
The other big strategy--
and this is personally

00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:35.910
my favorite--

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:39.040
that we've applied
to bring down costs

00:22:39.040 --> 00:22:42.560
is the use of preemptible VMs.

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:46.150
Preemptible VMs are
machines where you get them

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:47.650
at a really steep discount.

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:52.019
You pay 20% of the normal
price for a machine.

00:22:52.019 --> 00:22:53.560
But that comes with
the understanding

00:22:53.560 --> 00:22:57.040
that you can get kicked off
the machine at any time.

00:22:57.040 --> 00:23:00.280
So for really long running
jobs, that's not so desirable.

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:02.800
But if you're running
really short jobs

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:07.180
or up to a few hours, it's
actually a really good deal,

00:23:07.180 --> 00:23:10.820
because you're paying a
lot less for the compute.

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:12.490
And there's some
logic built in so

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:14.770
that if you do get
kicked off the machine,

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:16.580
there will be some
automatic retries.

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:19.420
And you can even tune
how many attempts

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:22.720
you want to do on preemptibles
before defaulting back

00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:27.370
to a regular machine
that you have for sure.

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:31.720
And so by doing that, and
by switching virtually

00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:35.440
all the steps in our pipelines
to use in preemptible VMs,

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:40.060
we've gained an
additional 35% savings

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:45.160
on the cost of running
our gene pipeline.

00:23:45.160 --> 00:23:51.220
And now, the final piece in the
puzzle, the last big strategy

00:23:51.220 --> 00:23:56.110
that we've applied to cut down
costs of our pipeline operation

00:23:56.110 --> 00:24:02.720
is to be really smart about
how we're accessing the data.

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:05.410
JP Martin at Verily
Life Sciences

00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:08.140
contributed some
really valuable code

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:11.080
to HTSJDK, which
is an open source

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:13.200
library that underlies GATK.

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:17.950
It's what GATK uses to read
in and write out files.

00:24:17.950 --> 00:24:27.640
And the code that JP contributed
allows us to read data directly

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:29.350
from the bucket
instead of having

00:24:29.350 --> 00:24:31.930
to copy the file
to the local disk

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:34.240
before we can compute on it.

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:37.190
And that makes a big difference,
because, for example,

00:24:37.190 --> 00:24:43.420
if we're parallelizing
across genomic regions,

00:24:43.420 --> 00:24:46.090
if you think that
normally you would

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:51.010
have to copy an entire genome
BAM file to local disk,

00:24:51.010 --> 00:24:53.950
but now you can
skip that and just

00:24:53.950 --> 00:24:56.590
copy the little piece of
data that you actually

00:24:56.590 --> 00:24:57.970
want to run on.

00:24:57.970 --> 00:25:01.680
That allows you
to go much faster,

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:05.410
because you don't have to
wait for 100 gig BAM file

00:25:05.410 --> 00:25:06.580
to get copied.

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:11.170
And it also allows you to
allocate a much smaller disk

00:25:11.170 --> 00:25:12.080
space.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:15.700
So that ties into the first
strategy that I mentioned,

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:20.950
where we can use much
smaller disks for each task.

00:25:20.950 --> 00:25:22.930
And if you're
thinking, well, this

00:25:22.930 --> 00:25:29.950
is going to be used across
thousands or tens of thousands

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:32.950
of genomic intervals,
and then replicated

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:37.690
across tens of thousands
of genomes themselves,

00:25:37.690 --> 00:25:41.500
that amounts to fairly
significant savings again.

00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:46.510
In this case, 15% savings on
the total cost of the pipeline.

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:49.720
And the results of all
of that, in aggregate,

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:54.880
is that from an initial
cost of $45 per genome

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:59.990
to process an entire
genome on Google Cloud,

00:25:59.990 --> 00:26:05.140
we've cut that down
to $5 per genome.

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:11.200
And we've done some additional
tweaking that I can share.

00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:14.670
We're seeing now that with some
of those additional tweaks,

00:26:14.670 --> 00:26:17.920
we can actually bring down the
cost of the pipeline right now

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:21.460
to $3.65 per genome.

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:25.630
So that just gives
us so much more bang

00:26:25.630 --> 00:26:27.430
for our research buck.

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:30.130
It has made a huge
difference in terms

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:33.700
of what we can do at
the Broad Institute

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:36.820
with our genomic pipelines.

00:26:36.820 --> 00:26:43.210
I want to be clear that we're
still offering best practices

00:26:43.210 --> 00:26:46.120
pipelines that are generic
that can run anywhere,

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:51.010
but we have this version
of the GATK4 pipelines

00:26:51.010 --> 00:26:53.360
that is very
optimized, very deeply

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:54.710
optimized for Google Cloud.

00:26:54.710 --> 00:26:56.420
It's what we run in production.

00:26:56.420 --> 00:26:59.440
We make that available so
that everybody can benefit

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:01.720
from this optimizing work.

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:04.240
You can try this
pipeline in FireCloud.

00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:07.600
It's preloaded in
the workspace that

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:12.250
lives at the URL that's on your
screen, the $5 genome analysis

00:27:12.250 --> 00:27:13.910
pipeline, which we
might have to have

00:27:13.910 --> 00:27:17.630
to update the name of to
the $3 genome pipeline.

00:27:17.630 --> 00:27:19.960
And hopefully, that will
just keep going down.

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:25.330
So that covers the
work that we've done

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:27.840
so far with GATK and FireCloud.

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:31.600
I'm going to throw it over to my
friend Keith from Google, who's

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:34.080
going to talk about
platform innovation.

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:40.350
KEITH BINDER: Great,
thanks Geraldine.

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:44.130
So innovation is something
that we hold dearly at Google.

00:27:44.130 --> 00:27:49.200
And as you heard from
Jonathan and Geraldine,

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:53.060
our innovation stems from
things like the Pipelines API,

00:27:53.060 --> 00:27:59.590
to BigQuery, even to stuff
like preemptible VMs.

00:27:59.590 --> 00:28:02.620
It's fascinating to
think that for the price

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:06.550
of a cup of coffee in New York,
you can do an entire genome

00:28:06.550 --> 00:28:07.300
analysis.

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:10.106
To me, that's amazing.

00:28:10.106 --> 00:28:12.590
But our innovation
doesn't just stop there.

00:28:15.770 --> 00:28:19.220
At Google, we don't
believe you should simply

00:28:19.220 --> 00:28:24.080
lift your workload or
application into the cloud.

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:28.580
We believe that you
should lift, shift,

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:33.170
and improve upon that
workload and application.

00:28:33.170 --> 00:28:35.420
And the improvements
take form of some

00:28:35.420 --> 00:28:38.210
of the cost innovation
and technical innovation

00:28:38.210 --> 00:28:39.900
that we have.

00:28:39.900 --> 00:28:43.670
For instance, we have a
live migration capability.

00:28:43.670 --> 00:28:47.570
So your instances can
seamlessly migrate

00:28:47.570 --> 00:28:50.780
to a machine during
maintenance windows

00:28:50.780 --> 00:28:53.660
without any user intervention
and without any planned

00:28:53.660 --> 00:28:54.960
downtime.

00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:56.720
This is something
we took advantage

00:28:56.720 --> 00:29:01.960
of during the recent Specter
meltdown vulnerabilities,

00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:04.760
whereby we migrated
our entire customer

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:09.980
environment seamlessly, without
them having to be involved,

00:29:09.980 --> 00:29:13.640
in order to address
that vulnerability.

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:16.820
Additionally, we have
custom machine types.

00:29:16.820 --> 00:29:19.820
So we know that
applications sometimes

00:29:19.820 --> 00:29:24.980
don't fall into a specific
ratio of CPU and memory.

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:28.430
So we allow you to define
what that ratio is.

00:29:28.430 --> 00:29:34.000
Maybe you need four
cores and 80 gig of RAM.

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:37.200
We don't stop you
from doing that.

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:40.580
Additionally, we
have recommendations

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:44.440
using our vast history
of machine learning

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:46.760
to look at how an
instance is performing

00:29:46.760 --> 00:29:50.450
and say, you know
what, if you change

00:29:50.450 --> 00:29:55.010
the core count on this machine,
or maybe reduce the memory

00:29:55.010 --> 00:29:58.280
footprint, not only will
your instance still run,

00:29:58.280 --> 00:30:02.990
but you'll save a
couple dollars as well.

00:30:02.990 --> 00:30:07.190
We believe that you should
pay for what you consume.

00:30:07.190 --> 00:30:09.650
And to that end, we
have per second billing.

00:30:09.650 --> 00:30:13.130
So if your instance
runs for 73 seconds,

00:30:13.130 --> 00:30:15.200
you're going to
pay for 73 seconds.

00:30:15.200 --> 00:30:19.730
You're not going to pay for
120 seconds, or even more.

00:30:19.730 --> 00:30:23.930
We also have extremely fast
startups for our instances.

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:26.070
We're talking about
a matter of seconds.

00:30:26.070 --> 00:30:30.240
So this gets you up and
running as fast as possible.

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:34.130
And lastly, we allow you to
resize your disks on the fly.

00:30:34.130 --> 00:30:35.810
If you need a bigger
disk, we allow

00:30:35.810 --> 00:30:38.120
you to do that
seamlessly, as well

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:42.200
as increasing the IOPS for that
disk as you make it bigger.

00:30:45.990 --> 00:30:51.120
Now, innovation isn't only
about technology and cost.

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:55.295
We think security is also a
strategic part of innovation

00:30:55.295 --> 00:30:56.630
at Google Cloud.

00:30:56.630 --> 00:31:00.110
And it starts with
you, the customer.

00:31:00.110 --> 00:31:04.370
By default, without you
having to do anything,

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:09.170
all of your data in transit
and at rest is encrypted.

00:31:09.170 --> 00:31:12.300
You don't have to worry
about any PKI infrastructure.

00:31:12.300 --> 00:31:15.380
You don't have to worry
about any key management.

00:31:15.380 --> 00:31:18.590
Now, of course,
if you are a type

00:31:18.590 --> 00:31:22.040
that likes to manage your own
keys, we allow you to do that.

00:31:22.040 --> 00:31:26.480
But knowing that you
don't have to do that

00:31:26.480 --> 00:31:31.150
is an extremely, extremely
important benefit.

00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:32.900
And this notion of
security and encryption

00:31:32.900 --> 00:31:37.500
extends into our cloud stack.

00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:40.780
It starts at the silicon level.

00:31:40.780 --> 00:31:45.600
We made our own
in-house secure chip

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:48.270
that we put on every
peripheral device.

00:31:48.270 --> 00:31:51.270
And that secure chip will
check to make sure it's

00:31:51.270 --> 00:31:56.640
a trusted root during boot.

00:31:56.640 --> 00:32:01.020
If it detects any abnormality
during the manufacturing cycle,

00:32:01.020 --> 00:32:04.950
or even during steady state,
that machine in our data center

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:10.170
will not boot and will be
reported to our engineers.

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:15.280
That extends into our
server environment.

00:32:15.280 --> 00:32:21.730
We have our own custom
servers that we manufacture.

00:32:21.730 --> 00:32:26.290
And having the ability
to make our own servers,

00:32:26.290 --> 00:32:28.840
we eliminate all
the extraneous stuff

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:32.380
that you may have in a
server from off the shelf.

00:32:32.380 --> 00:32:34.840
You don't necessarily
have to put in video cards

00:32:34.840 --> 00:32:36.500
if they're not needed.

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:39.820
So not only do we free
up valuable real estate

00:32:39.820 --> 00:32:44.410
on that server to introduce
additional CPU and RAM,

00:32:44.410 --> 00:32:49.420
but we're actually
eliminating a threat vector

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:52.240
by eliminating potential
vulnerabilities

00:32:52.240 --> 00:32:56.980
in those peripheral devices
that you don't even need.

00:32:56.980 --> 00:32:59.620
We also extend this to
our storage environment

00:32:59.620 --> 00:33:01.710
and our network environment.

00:33:01.710 --> 00:33:04.150
They are both
custom and allow us

00:33:04.150 --> 00:33:07.450
to own not only the
hardware, but the software

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:10.280
and the firmware stack as well.

00:33:10.280 --> 00:33:13.510
And lastly, at a holistic
level, our data centers

00:33:13.510 --> 00:33:16.630
have both logical and
physical security.

00:33:16.630 --> 00:33:19.750
To that end, because we own
the hardware, the software,

00:33:19.750 --> 00:33:23.380
and the firmware, are not
reliant upon any third party

00:33:23.380 --> 00:33:28.030
to provide patches in
case of a vulnerability.

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:30.280
As we control it, we
can issue those patches

00:33:30.280 --> 00:33:33.500
as soon as possible.

00:33:33.500 --> 00:33:36.410
Now, you don't have to
take our word for it.

00:33:36.410 --> 00:33:41.920
We do annual audits with
our third party consultants

00:33:41.920 --> 00:33:47.300
to test the attestation
of our environment.

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:52.670
And when it comes to genomics
and the life sciences,

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:56.450
that one certification that
you may see on the bottom left

00:33:56.450 --> 00:33:57.720
is of utmost importance.

00:33:57.720 --> 00:33:59.120
And that's HIPAA.

00:33:59.120 --> 00:34:01.850
With Google, our entire
cloud environment

00:34:01.850 --> 00:34:04.790
is HIPAA certified,
which means you

00:34:04.790 --> 00:34:08.400
don't have to worry about
selecting a certain cloud

00:34:08.400 --> 00:34:11.600
or instance to
put that PHI data.

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:13.639
As long as you put
it on Google Cloud,

00:34:13.639 --> 00:34:19.219
it's automatically covered
by our HIPAA certification.

00:34:19.219 --> 00:34:22.610
And you can see, we have a vast
amount of other certifications

00:34:22.610 --> 00:34:27.590
as well, such as PCI,
FedRAMP, et cetera.

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:37.520
And lastly, innovation is
not about keeping all of it

00:34:37.520 --> 00:34:38.929
close to the chest.

00:34:38.929 --> 00:34:44.907
We are a firm proponent of
the open source community.

00:34:44.907 --> 00:34:48.969
To that end, we've incubated
numerous projects within Google

00:34:48.969 --> 00:34:52.260
that we've released to the
open source environment.

00:34:52.260 --> 00:34:54.480
Two of those projects
in particular,

00:34:54.480 --> 00:34:57.370
Kubernetes for
container orchestration

00:34:57.370 --> 00:35:00.400
and TensorFlow for
machine learning,

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:03.490
are the number one and
number two highest engagement

00:35:03.490 --> 00:35:05.410
in all of GitHub.

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:09.740
And not only do we incubate
products and release

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:13.030
into the open source
community, but our employees,

00:35:13.030 --> 00:35:17.020
our Googlers, also are
active contributors.

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:21.160
As of 2017, there
are 1,100 projects

00:35:21.160 --> 00:35:24.880
in GitHub contributed
by Googlers.

00:35:24.880 --> 00:35:29.410
And those Googlers totaled
over 900 employees.

00:35:38.200 --> 00:35:41.960
So stay tuned for a live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:42.460
minute.

00:36:37.420 --> 00:36:39.354
JONATHAN SHEFFI: All
right, we're back.

00:36:39.354 --> 00:36:41.770
KEITH BINDER: All right, some
questions from the audience.

00:36:41.770 --> 00:36:45.610
So I've been told this first
question comes from a Jesus

00:36:45.610 --> 00:36:49.030
Gomez in New York,
who asks, do I

00:36:49.030 --> 00:36:52.060
have to bid for
preemptible instances?

00:36:52.060 --> 00:36:55.360
JONATHAN SHEFFI: Yeah,
the answer is no.

00:36:55.360 --> 00:36:57.470
This is a big difference
with Google Cloud

00:36:57.470 --> 00:37:00.220
in that you don't have to bid
for preemptible instances.

00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:01.870
You just select them.

00:37:01.870 --> 00:37:04.360
And Pipelines API
provides an option for you

00:37:04.360 --> 00:37:09.270
to employ both preemptible
CPUs and preemptible GPUs.

00:37:09.270 --> 00:37:12.450
You just use them, you get the
discount, and you go to work.

00:37:12.450 --> 00:37:15.130
KEITH BINDER: And that's a
flat 80% off for all instances.

00:37:15.130 --> 00:37:17.740
And GPUs is a flat 50%.

00:37:17.740 --> 00:37:21.680
So definitely take
advantage of that.

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:23.620
How do I get started
with FireCloud?

00:37:23.620 --> 00:37:26.890
JONATHAN SHEFFI: Yeah,
FireCloud.org is the URL

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:28.060
that you're looking for.

00:37:28.060 --> 00:37:29.980
And you can make an account.

00:37:29.980 --> 00:37:33.640
You actually get $250 in
credits just to get started,

00:37:33.640 --> 00:37:36.130
so that's quite a few
genomes, as you've

00:37:36.130 --> 00:37:39.100
heard about some of the
really cool cost work that's

00:37:39.100 --> 00:37:42.430
being done by Broad.

00:37:42.430 --> 00:37:44.740
Yeah.

00:37:44.740 --> 00:37:48.280
KEITH BINDER: And I
have a GCP instance.

00:37:48.280 --> 00:37:51.070
I know FireCloud runs on GCP.

00:37:51.070 --> 00:37:54.400
Do I have to create a
new billing account?

00:37:54.400 --> 00:37:59.050
How do I get my
procurement team involved?

00:37:59.050 --> 00:38:01.154
Can I use my existing
account with FireCloud?

00:38:01.154 --> 00:38:02.570
JONATHAN SHEFFI:
You actually can.

00:38:02.570 --> 00:38:05.110
And there's instructions on
how to do this both on our blog

00:38:05.110 --> 00:38:06.830
and on the FireCloud site.

00:38:06.830 --> 00:38:09.520
So you can bring your
own GCP billing account.

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:11.860
You can supply the
billing account ID,

00:38:11.860 --> 00:38:14.080
and then the charge
for all that compute

00:38:14.080 --> 00:38:15.790
goes to your home institution.

00:38:15.790 --> 00:38:18.180
So you're really getting all
the benefits of FireCloud.

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:22.030
You're getting all the
benefits of the optimizations.

00:38:22.030 --> 00:38:24.550
And yet, your home institution
is getting charged,

00:38:24.550 --> 00:38:29.860
so you don't have to worry about
doing a transaction with Broad.

00:38:29.860 --> 00:38:32.440
KEITH BINDER: Excellent.

00:38:32.440 --> 00:38:37.705
Does the $5 genome include
alignment, or is this GATK?

00:38:37.705 --> 00:38:38.330
Great question.

00:38:38.330 --> 00:38:39.663
JONATHAN SHEFFI: Great question.

00:38:39.663 --> 00:38:42.610
So the answer is yes.

00:38:42.610 --> 00:38:48.140
So $5 gets you, yes,
that's FASTQ to VCF.

00:38:48.140 --> 00:38:49.540
It's the quality metrics.

00:38:49.540 --> 00:38:52.390
So it's alignment
and variant calling.

00:38:52.390 --> 00:38:53.570
It's really exciting.

00:38:53.570 --> 00:38:57.450
I mean, while we're talking
about secondary analysis,

00:38:57.450 --> 00:39:00.440
there is so much innovation
happening in this field,

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:03.140
especially from a
cost perspective.

00:39:03.140 --> 00:39:06.874
We see GATK-- it is really funny
to think that it was really

00:39:06.874 --> 00:39:09.040
just a couple of years ago
that it was 10 times more

00:39:09.040 --> 00:39:09.820
expensive--

00:39:09.820 --> 00:39:11.709
9 to 10 times more expensive.

00:39:11.709 --> 00:39:13.000
And now we're talking about it.

00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:15.700
I think a lot of the
buzz in our industry

00:39:15.700 --> 00:39:17.800
is always about
the $1,000 genome

00:39:17.800 --> 00:39:20.600
and being able to
generate the data cheaply.

00:39:20.600 --> 00:39:22.290
But bringing down
the cost by 10x

00:39:22.290 --> 00:39:26.080
is pretty significant too
on the informatics side.

00:39:26.080 --> 00:39:29.270
So I think that as
we watch this field,

00:39:29.270 --> 00:39:31.930
we're going to see the
cost of secondary analysis

00:39:31.930 --> 00:39:34.780
get closer and closer to
zero, which is something

00:39:34.780 --> 00:39:37.300
I'm personally excited about,
because I think it unleashes

00:39:37.300 --> 00:39:39.850
all kinds of new research
questions and opportunities.

00:39:39.850 --> 00:39:42.410
KEITH BINDER: It sounds like
we'll be at $3 very soon.

00:39:42.410 --> 00:39:43.451
JONATHAN SHEFFI: Exactly.

